{
 "lead": "A uniform array has the narrowest beam for its length, but its first sidelobe is only about 13 dB down, too high for a radar that must ignore a strong echo off to one side. Giving the centre elements more current than the edge elements, an <b>amplitude taper</b>, lowers the sidelobes and widens the beam. The binomial taper removes the sidelobes completely; the Dolph-Chebyshev taper holds every sidelobe at one chosen level and gives the narrowest beam for that level. A progressive phase step, set electronically, then steers the beam: that is a <b>phased array</b>.",
 "prereq": "You need: the N-element array factor and the broadside and end-fire conditions from <a href=\"#uniform-linear-arrays-broadside-and-end-fire\">uniform linear arrays</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Binomial arrays",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Keep the elements on the z axis with spacing d, angle θ from the axis and ψ = βd cosθ + α. With amplitudes a<sub>n</sub> the array factor is AF = Σ a<sub>n</sub> e<sup>jnψ</sup>. The binomial array copies the two-element pattern N − 1 times over: (1 + e<sup>jψ</sup>)<sup>N−1</sup>. Expanding the power gives the amplitudes as the rows of Pascal’s triangle."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "a<sub>n</sub> = C(N − 1, n), n = 0 … N − 1",
     "where": "C(N − 1, n) is “N − 1 choose n”: 1, 1 for N = 2; 1, 2, 1 for N = 3; 1, 3, 3, 1 for N = 4; 1, 4, 6, 4, 1 for N = 5."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "|AF| = |2 cos(ψ/2)|<sup>N−1</sup>",
     "where": "The normalized pattern is |cos(ψ/2)|<sup>N−1</sup>. For d = λ/2 and α = 0 the visible range is −π ≤ ψ ≤ π, where |cos(ψ/2)| falls from 1 to 0 with no second peak."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "So a broadside binomial array with d ≤ λ/2 has no sidelobes at all. The price is a wide beam and low directivity: for N = 5 the half-power beamwidth is 30.3° (the estimate 1.06/√(N − 1) rad gives 30.4°), against 20.8° for the uniform array of the same length, and the edge currents shrink fast (1/6 of the centre for N = 5)."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Dolph-Chebyshev arrays",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Dolph saw that the Chebyshev polynomial T<sub>m</sub>(x) = cos(m cos⁻¹x) swings between −1 and +1 for |x| ≤ 1 and then rises steeply. If the array factor of an N-element symmetric broadside array is made equal to T<sub>N−1</sub>(x<sub>0</sub> cos u), with u = ψ/2, the swings become sidelobes of height 1 and the steep part becomes a main beam of height R. Every sidelobe is then 1/R of the peak, and no taper gives a narrower first-null beamwidth for that level."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "R = 10<sup>SLL/20</sup>",
     "where": "R is the main-beam to sidelobe voltage ratio; SLL is how far the sidelobes sit below the peak in dB, taken as a positive number. A −20 dB sidelobe gives R = 10; −30 dB gives R = 31.6."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "x<sub>0</sub> = cosh[cosh⁻¹(R)/(N − 1)]",
     "where": "x<sub>0</sub> is the point where T<sub>N−1</sub>(x<sub>0</sub>) = R; it maps the main-beam peak (u = 0)."
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Find R from the sidelobe level and x<sub>0</sub> from the formula above.",
      "Write the array factor of the symmetric array as a cosine series: for odd N = 2M + 1, AF = I<sub>0</sub> + 2I<sub>1</sub> cos 2u + … + 2I<sub>M</sub> cos 2Mu, where I<sub>0</sub> is the centre current.",
      "Write each cos 2ku as powers of cos u, put cos u = x/x<sub>0</sub>, and match the coefficients of T<sub>N−1</sub>(x), highest power first.",
      "Normalize the currents, usually to the edge element."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Phased arrays: steering with the phase step",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The beam of a uniform-phase-step array sits where ψ = 0. To point it at θ<sub>0</sub> from the axis, set the step α so that ψ is zero there. Each element gets its phase from a phase shifter fed by a power divider; changing the shifter settings moves the beam in microseconds with nothing turning. Phase-shifter hardware belongs to the microwave components topic."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "α = −βd cosθ<sub>0</sub>",
     "where": "θ<sub>0</sub> is the scan direction measured from the array axis (θ<sub>0</sub> = 90° is broadside). Radar books often measure the scan angle θ<sub>s</sub> = 90° − θ<sub>0</sub> from broadside; then α = −βd sinθ<sub>s</sub>."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "d/λ < 1/(1 + |cosθ<sub>0</sub>|)",
     "where": "The condition for no grating lobe in visible space when the beam is steered to θ<sub>0</sub>. At broadside it allows d < λ; at end-fire it requires d < λ/2. So d = λ/2 is safe for every scan angle short of end-fire."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Phase shifters give a fixed phase, not a fixed time delay, so the beam moves when the frequency changes: cosθ<sub>0</sub>(f) = −αc/(2πfd). This is <b>beam squint</b>. A beam set 40° off broadside at 10 GHz sits only 32.4° off broadside at 12 GHz. True-time-delay lines or digital beamforming remove it in wideband systems."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "Design a 5-element broadside Dolph-Chebyshev array with d = λ/2 and sidelobes 20 dB below the main beam. Find the excitation currents, the first nulls, and compare the beamwidth and directivity with the uniform and binomial arrays of five elements.",
   "steps": [
    "R = 10<sup>20/20</sup> = 10; N − 1 = 4. cosh⁻¹10 = 2.9932, so x<sub>0</sub> = cosh(2.9932/4) = cosh(0.7483) = 1.2933.",
    "For N = 5 (M = 2): AF = I<sub>0</sub> + 2I<sub>1</sub> cos 2u + 2I<sub>2</sub> cos 4u, with cos 2u = 2cos²u − 1 and cos 4u = 8cos⁴u − 8cos²u + 1.",
    "T<sub>4</sub>(x) = 8x⁴ − 8x² + 1 with x = x<sub>0</sub> cos u. Match cos⁴u: 16I<sub>2</sub> = 8x<sub>0</sub>⁴, so I<sub>2</sub> = x<sub>0</sub>⁴/2 = 2.7976/2 = 1.3988.",
    "Match cos²u: 4I<sub>1</sub> − 16I<sub>2</sub> = −8x<sub>0</sub>², so I<sub>1</sub> = 4I<sub>2</sub> − 2x<sub>0</sub>² = 5.5952 − 3.3452 = 2.2500.",
    "Match the constant: I<sub>0</sub> − 2I<sub>1</sub> + 2I<sub>2</sub> = 1, so I<sub>0</sub> = 1 + 4.5000 − 2.7976 = 2.7024.",
    "Check the peak: AF(u = 0) = 2.7024 + 2(2.2500) + 2(1.3988) = 10.0 = R. Divide by the edge current: 1 : 1.609 : 1.932 : 1.609 : 1.",
    "First nulls: T<sub>4</sub> = 0 first at x = cos(π/8) = 0.9239, so cos u = 0.9239/1.2933 = 0.7144, u = 0.775 rad, ψ = 1.550 rad, cosθ = 1.550/π = 0.493, θ = 60.4° and 119.6°.",
    {
     "text": "Compare (pattern computed for each taper):",
     "table": {
      "head": [
       "Five elements, d = λ/2",
       "Currents",
       "Sidelobe",
       "HPBW",
       "D"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "Uniform",
        "1 : 1 : 1 : 1 : 1",
        "−12.0 dB",
        "20.8°",
        "5.0 (7.0 dBi)"
       ],
       [
        "Dolph-Chebyshev",
        "1 : 1.609 : 1.932 : 1.609 : 1",
        "−20.0 dB",
        "23.7°",
        "4.69 (6.7 dBi)"
       ],
       [
        "Binomial",
        "1 : 4 : 6 : 4 : 1",
        "none",
        "30.3°",
        "3.66 (5.6 dBi)"
       ]
      ]
     }
    }
   ],
   "answer": "Currents 1 : 1.609 : 1.932 : 1.609 : 1, first nulls at 60.4° and 119.6° (FNBW 59.1°). The −20 dB Chebyshev beam (23.7°) is only a little wider than the uniform beam (20.8°) and far narrower than the binomial beam (30.3°)."
  },
  {
   "question": "A 4-element phased array with d = λ/2 and equal amplitudes must point its beam 30° off broadside toward +z. Find the phase step and the element phases, and check that no grating lobe appears.",
   "steps": [
    "30° off broadside toward +z is θ<sub>0</sub> = 60° from the axis. βd = π.",
    "α = −βd cosθ<sub>0</sub> = −π × 0.5 = −π/2 rad = −90°.",
    "Element phases: 0°, −90°, −180°, −270° (the last is the same setting as +90°).",
    "Grating-lobe check: 1/(1 + |cos 60°|) = 1/1.5 = 0.667, and d/λ = 0.5 < 0.667. Safe.",
    "Check: at θ = 60°, ψ = π(0.5) − π/2 = 0, so all four waves add."
   ],
   "answer": "α = −90°; phases 0°, −90°, −180°, −270°; no grating lobe, since d/λ = 0.5 is below the limit 0.667."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Saying a Chebyshev array has no sidelobes. It has sidelobes, all at the chosen level; the binomial array is the one with none, and only for d ≤ λ/2.",
  "Reading R as 0.1 for a −20 dB sidelobe. R is the main-beam to sidelobe ratio: 10<sup>20/20</sup> = 10.",
  "Using N instead of N − 1 in x<sub>0</sub> and T<sub>N−1</sub>. Five elements need T<sub>4</sub>.",
  "Mixing scan-angle references. From the axis α = −βd cosθ<sub>0</sub>; from broadside α = −βd sinθ<sub>s</sub>. The same 30°-off-broadside beam needs −90° either way.",
  "Blaming the taper for a grating lobe. Grating lobes come from spacing and scan angle; check d/λ < 1/(1 + |cosθ<sub>0</sub>|)."
 ],
