 "beyond": "Not for the exam. Long Chebyshev designs become impractical because the edge currents jump up; Taylor’s distribution, which lets the far sidelobes fall away, is used instead in most radar antennas. Active electronically scanned arrays put a transmit-receive module with its own phase shifter and amplifier behind every element, and digital beamforming replaces analogue phase shifters with per-element sampling. Search for Taylor distribution, AESA radar and true-time-delay beamforming.",
 "figure": {
